title: A sealed envelope
subtitle: Clara Zetkin writes about papers Moscow would rather keep shut.
new-page: true
tags: kpd_event
view-if: year = 1923 and month = 4 and test_stage = 0 and civil_war = 0

= A letter from Clara Zetkin

The letter comes in April, by a courier and not through the Comintern's post, and the envelope is addressed in Clara Zetkin's small, upright hand. She has known the Russian leaders since before the war, and she is told more in Moscow than she is supposed to repeat. She repeats a little of it to us.

Lenin has not spoken since his third stroke in March. In the last weeks before it he dictated notes to his secretaries about the future of the Russian party. They were sealed, and now they lie with his wife, Nadezhda Krupskaya, who is meant to hand them to a party congress when the time comes. A few men in the Politburo know what is in them. Some of those few would be happy never to see the envelope opened.

Zetkin tells us that the notes are about the leaders, and that one of them is not spared. She does not say which. She writes only that an old communist, who does not think a party's fate should be settled in a secretary's office, might want a copy to be read in Berlin. The rest is for us to decide.

It is a strange thing to be offered. We would be keeping a secret that belongs to the Russian party, and holding it would make us a party to the fight over who comes after Lenin. But nobody offers a paper like this to people they do not trust.

- @ask: Send for the courier.
- @decline: Leave it alone.

@decline
