We write back that the German party asks the Russian party for nothing it does not choose to tell us. Zetkin's answer is short and not unfriendly. The Centre is relieved to hear that we want no part in Moscow's quarrels. The envelope stays with Krupskaya, and whatever is in it will be read, if it is read at all, by other people.

@ask
subtitle: dev: test_stage = 1
title: Lenin's last notes
on-arrival: test_stage = 1

= What the notes say

The courier comes after midnight with a thin packet wrapped in oilcloth. Inside are typed copies of what Lenin dictated in the last days of December and the first days of January. We read them in the Zentrale with the shutters closed and a pot of coffee going cold.

The first paper is a letter to the next party congress. Lenin wants the Central Committee enlarged to fifty or a hundred members, so that the party is not run by a handful of men whose quarrels could split it. He says the greatest danger of a split lies in the relations between two of them, Stalin and Trotsky, and then he sketches the leaders one by one. Stalin, having become General Secretary, has concentrated enormous power in his hands, and Lenin is not sure he always knows how to use it with enough caution. Trotsky is perhaps the most capable man on the committee, but too sure of himself and too taken with administration. Zinoviev and Kamenev's stand in October 1917 was no accident, though it can no more be held against them personally than Trotsky's old quarrels can be held against him. Bukharin is the party's most valuable theorist, but Lenin doubts that his views are fully Marxist.

The second paper is a postscript dated 4 January. Stalin, Lenin writes, is too rude. That is bearable among communists, and it becomes unbearable in a General Secretary. He asks the comrades to think of a way to remove Stalin from the post and to find a man who is more patient, more loyal, more polite, more considerate to his comrades and less capricious. A third paper, on the nationalities, accuses Stalin, Ordzhonikidze and Dzerzhinsky of Great-Russian chauvinism in the way they treated the Georgian communists.

Nobody speaks for a while. It is not a programme and it is not quite a verdict. It is a man who knows his time is short, trying to put his party in order before it runs out. The names in it are the names of the men who run the International, and what they do next will decide how much room the German party has left to breathe.

Brandler and Thalheimer are the first to answer. They have always believed the party's strength is in its workers, and not in the corridors of the Comintern, and they would like a friend in Moscow who thinks the same. Bukharin is that kind of man, and so is Rykov, who chairs the Soviet government and keeps an eye on the peasants and the market. For them the paper is a way to find allies.

The Left is quicker and angrier. Fischer and Maslow want to be first to use it, and the men they want to use it for are Trotsky and Radek, who still talk of the German revolution as something that cannot wait. Thälmann says nothing at all. He is loyal to the secretary's office because the secretary's office has been loyal to him, and he looks like a man who has just been told that his friend is under suspicion.

Zetkin's letter said a copy might be read here. It did not say what to do with it afterwards. Whatever we do, Moscow will know within weeks.

- @left: Take it to Radek.
- @right: Take it to Bukharin.
- @publish: Print it.
- @hold: Lock it in the safe.

