Radek is the party's friend in the Comintern and Trotsky's in the Politburo, and a messenger gets the copy to him within the week. He reads it twice, folds it into his jacket, and says only that it must be used at the right moment and that he will not be the one to use it.

Within days the Left Opposition has a weapon. In the Russian party the balance tips against the secretaries, and the slow drift toward Stalin comes to a stop. Nothing is decided; the Central Committee has not even met. Our own Left takes the credit and grows stronger for it, and Brandler's wing does not forgive us for throwing a lighted match into the Russian party without asking first.

