Brandler's friends have a line to Bukharin, and through him to Rykov and Tomsky, who run the state and the trade unions. None of them want a Secretariat that governs the party by its appointments, and a dying man's opinion, written down while he could still think, is exactly what they need.

In the Russian party the balance slips against the secretaries and the slow drift toward Stalin stops. Nothing is decided; the Central Committee has not met. Our own Right is pleased with itself, because it can now claim the friendship of the men who will matter, while the Left, which wanted the paper for a different man, is furious.

