The extracts go into our press on a Sunday, under a headline that never names the man they are about. By Monday every other paper in Berlin has them, and Vorwärts prints them beside a leading article about dictatorship inside the Russian party. The men whose names are in them read them in the German press.

The Centre, whose loyalty is to the secretariat that has been loyal to it, is dismayed. Both oppositions, in Berlin and in Moscow, take heart, and the balance in the Russian party moves against the secretaries, though nothing is decided before the Central Committee meets. Moscow does not take it quietly, and our standing with the Comintern drops sharply.

@hold
